course: dictionary
lesson: 15261
title: Shush and being quiet
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Shush is a word that asks someone to be quiet. Read the clues around it: «Shush! The baby is finally asleep.» The exclamation mark and the sleeping baby tell you someone wants the room to be silent.
explain: Shush can also be a verb, so it changes like other verbs: «The librarian shushed the noisy children.» Today she shushes, yesterday she shushed, and right now she is shushing them.
explain: Try the swap check. Put «be quiet» into the sentence: «Be quiet! The baby is finally asleep.» It still makes sense, so shush means be quiet.
fact: Shush means be quiet.
fact: «Shush!» is an interjection, a word said on its own to tell someone to stop talking.
fact: The verb forms are shush, shushes, shushed and shushing.
fact: People often say «shh» with a finger on their lips, and it means the same thing as shush.
fact: Libraries, cinemas and bedtime are everyday places where people hear shush.
exercise: «Shush! The baby has just gone to sleep.» What does shush mean here? | Wake up and say hello | *Be quiet | Sing a happy song | Run away fast
exercise: Which word is the past tense of shush? | shushes | shushing | *shushed | shush
exercise: Where would you most often hear someone say «Shush!»? | *In a library or cinema | On a football pitch, cheering a goal | At the top of a mountain | In a shop that sells shoes
test: «Shush now, Ella, or you will wake your sister.» What does shush now mean? | Shout for help | Get dressed quickly | Please sing louder | *Be quiet now
test: Every night, Mum ___ the children when they giggle in bed. Which word fits? | shushed | shushing | *shushes | shush
exam: Which sentence uses shush correctly? | «Shush the ball over the net.» | «The shush was painted bright red.» | «We shushed the ball into the goal.» | *«Shush, the teacher is starting the lesson.»
